Name: replaceText

Type: function

Syntax: replaceText(<stringToChange>, <matchExpression>, <replacementString>)

Summary:
Searches for a <regular expression> and replaces the portions that match
the <regular expression>.

Introduced: 1.0.0

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop, server

Example:
put replaceText("malformed","mal","well") -- returns "wellformed"

Example:
-- change return-delimited text to comma-delimited
put replaceText(field "Stats",return,comma) into field "Stats"

Example:
put replaceText("colour or color","colou?r","culler")
-- returns "culler or culler"

Example:
-- escape RegEx metacharacter using backslash
put replaceText ( "ABC|DEV" , "\|" , CR )

Example:
-- reuse the text of parenthesized groups in the replacement
put replaceText("John Smith", "(\w+) (\w+)", "$2, $1")
-- returns "Smith, John"

Example:
-- refer to named groups by name
put replaceText("2026-11-24", "(?<y>\d+)-(?<m>\d+)-(?<d>\d+)", "${d}/${m}/${y}")
-- returns "24/11/2026"

Parameters:
stringToChange (string): A container reference or literal value.

matchExpression:
A regular expression.

replacementString (string): A container reference or literal value.


Returns:
The <replaceText> function returns the changed <string>.

Description:
Use the <replaceText> function to search for and replace text that
matches a particular pattern.

The <replaceText> function replaces all the occurrences of the
<matchExpression> with the <replacementString>. If more than one
matching substring is found, the <replaceText> function replaces all of
them. 

The search for each match begins where the previous match ended. The
text before that point is still taken into account: "^" matches only at
the real start of the <stringToChange> (or of a line, with the "(?m)"
option), and "\b" and lookbehind assertions see the characters that
precede the match. A <matchExpression> that can match no characters,
such as "x*", is replaced at each position where it matches nothing.

The <replacementString> can refer to the text matched by the
parenthesized groups of the <matchExpression>:

- "$1" or "\1" is the text of the first group, "$2" or "\2" the second,
  and so on. A two-digit number is used if the <matchExpression> has a
  group of that number.
- "$0" or "\0" is the whole of the matched text.
- "${1}" is the same as "$1", and is useful when a digit follows: 
  "${1}0" is the first group followed by a zero.
- "${name}" is the text of the group named with "(?<name>...)".

A group that took no part in the match is replaced by nothing. A
reference to a group number or name that the <matchExpression> does not
have is not a reference: it is left in the result as it is written. To
put a "$" or "\" into the result where it would otherwise begin a
reference, write "\$" or "\\".

By default a <regular expression> works on text one UTF-16 code unit at
a time and treats only ASCII characters as letters and digits. Two
switches, written at the very start of the <matchExpression>, change
this:

- "(*UTF)" makes "." and character classes treat a character outside the
  Basic Multilingual Plane, such as an emoji, as one character rather
  than two, and allows Unicode property classes such as "\p{Greek}".
- "(*UCP)" makes "\w", "\d", "\s", "\b" and the "(?i)" option follow
  Unicode rules, so that accented and non-Latin letters count as
  letters.

They can be combined, and must come before anything else, including
"(?i)": for example "(*UTF)(*UCP)(?i)\w+".

If the <matchExpression> is not valid, or matching it needs so many
steps that it is abandoned (which can happen with nested repeats such as
"(a+)+$"), the function throws an error.

If the <matchExpression> is not a valid <regular expression>, an error is thrown and
a description of what is wrong with it, and where (for example "missing closing
parenthesis at offset 3"), is placed in the result.

The <replaceText> function is not as fast as the <replace> <command>,
but is more flexible because you can search for any text that matches a
<regular expression>.

The <stringToChange> and <matchExpression> are always case-sensitive,
regardless of the setting of the <caseSensitive> <property>. (If you
need to make a case-insensitive comparison, use "(?i)" at the start of
the <matchExpression> to make the match case-insensitive.)

>*Note:* A number of characters in regular expressions have special
> meanings and these need to be 'escaped' using backslashes ("\"). For example
> period (".") matches any character, so in order to replace period characters
> using a <regular expression> use "\.", to replace vertical bar characters ("|")
> use "\|" and so on.
> For more information on <regular expressions> see the Perl documentation  
> at http://perldoc.perl.org/perlre.html .

Changes:
In version 0.9.16 the search for each match takes the preceding text
into account, matches of no characters are replaced, and the
<replacementString> can refer to groups. Before that, "^", "\b" and
lookbehinds were tested as if each search began a new string,
replacement stopped at the first match of no characters, and "$1", "\1",
"\$" and "\\" in the <replacementString> were ordinary text.

References: replace (command), filter (command), matchAll (function), command (glossary),
regular expression (glossary), property (glossary), string (keyword),
caseSensitive (property)

Tags: text processing
